INT. PICKLE CELLAR - GRANDMOTHER'S FARMHOUSE - LATE AFTERNOON

A low, cool stone room lined with shelves of jars. Dill, vinegar, garlic. A single bulb on a cord. Dust turns in the light.

NONNA FLORENTINA (80s, small, apron, forearms like rope) sorts jars by hand. Her grandson EMEKA (10, school uniform, backpack) sits on an upturned crate, swinging his legs. His mother TAMSIN (40s, scrubs, car keys in hand) stands on the bottom step.

She watches the old woman. She doesn't speak.

EMEKA
Nonna, why do you turn the jars?

NONNA FLORENTINA
So the brine moves. Stale brine makes soft pickles.

EMEKA
How do you know which ones are soft?

NONNA FLORENTINA
You listen. A good jar clicks.

She taps one with a fingernail. Holds it to his ear. He listens, frowns, nods.

TAMSIN
Mama, I have to get back to the hospital.

NONNA FLORENTINA
Then go.

TAMSIN
I told the school I'd pick him up at four.

NONNA FLORENTINA
And you did.

She doesn't turn around. Tamsin shifts on the step. Checks her watch. Puts it away.

EMEKA
Mum, can I stay for dinner?

TAMSIN
You have homework.

EMEKA
I can do it down here.

NONNA FLORENTINA
There is no light down here.

EMEKA
There's the bulb.

She smiles at that. She wipes her hands on her apron.

Upstairs, the screen door bangs. Footsteps cross the floor above. Dust drifts down from the beams. All three look up.

TAMSIN
Who's that?

NONNA FLORENTINA
Probably the man about the gutters.

Heavy boots on the cellar stairs. A shadow fills the doorway. A STRANGER (50s, work jacket, cap in hands, polite) ducks under the frame.

STRANGER
Sorry to bother you. Door was open.

TAMSIN
Can we help you?

STRANGER
I'm looking for Florentina Marchetti.

NONNA FLORENTINA
You found her.

He steps into the light. He looks at the jars. At the boy. At her.

STRANGER
My name's Bartosz. You knew my father. Janek.

She sets down the jar slowly. Her face does not change. Her hands do.

NONNA FLORENTINA
Janek the tinsmith.

BARTOSZ
That's the one.

TAMSIN
Mama?

NONNA FLORENTINA
It's all right, Tamsin.

She wipes her hands again. Longer this time.

NONNA FLORENTINA (CONT'D)
He used to come down here in August. Said my brine was the only thing in the county worth a hungry man's trouble.

BARTOSZ
He said that about you, too.

EMEKA
Who is he?

NONNA FLORENTINA
Someone's son.

She lifts a jar from the shelf, wipes the lid, and holds it out to Bartosz.

NONNA FLORENTINA (CONT'D)
Listen.

He takes it. Holds it to his ear. The cellar is very quiet.

BARTOSZ
It clicks.

NONNA FLORENTINA
Good. It's yours.

Tamsin steps down at last. Sits beside her son on the crate. Neither of them says anything. The bulb sways a little, then settles.
